  "id": "2026-09-23-editing-structural-rule-admission",
  "scope": "editing",
  "kind": "review",
  "date": "2026-09-23",
  "question": "Which insertion, deletion, normalization and escape rules belong to canonical editing commands rather than feature-specific repair?",
  "model": "GPT-6 / Codex",
  "method": "best-api-review/history-v3",
  "trigger": "User challenged canApplyBlockChanges in heading and blockquote Markdown rules after the required-prefix repair.",
  "requirements": [
    "An admitted input rule consumes marker and typed input in one schema-valid change; a declined conversion preserves both and delegates the original command.",
    "The actual semantic command and canonical schema own admission across root and nested content, configured identities and validation; a feature rule must not maintain a second document model.",
    "Keep the existing declarative rule priority, read-only matching and one-command transaction semantics.",
    "Do not accept added hot-path machinery or a performance claim without a matched executable comparison."
  ],
  "evidenceReuse": "Reused the September 21 atomic-rule direction and the September 22 required-prefix interaction proof. Re-read the current helper, both rule callers, Plite block admission and schema queries, the rule executor, generic block and list rules, focused tests and the repair plan. Earlier proof covers required-title heading and quote fallback; no fresh runtime or browser run was made for other rules.",
  "summary": "Pursue deleting the feature-owned shadow-document validator. It reconstructs and validates a guessed replacement before the real rule deletes the marker and issues a separate command. This fixed two prefix interactions, but admission and success remain split: Plite block commands can silently decline, while generic block/list rules delete markers first. Design one atomic accept-or-decline path at the Plite transaction/command boundary and let Plate rules fall through on decline. Keep canonical schema.content.prefix and the declarative rule system; do not approve a particular speculative API or its scale cost without design and proof.",
  "verdict": "pursue",
  "previous": "2026-09-22-editing-document-structure-post-implementation",
  "relation": "reaffirms",
  "reconciliation": [
    {
      "record": "2026-09-22-editing-document-structure-post-implementation",
      "question": "Does the implemented fixed-prefix schema satisfy complete, atomic editing and its stated proof gates?",
      "action": "retains",
      "reason": "Its canonical Plite schema owner and atomic accept-or-decline requirement remain correct. This review challenges only the subsequent feature-owned preflight implementation."
    },
    {
      "record": "2026-09-22-editing-document-structure-repair-execution",
      "question": "Which insertion, deletion, normalization and escape rules belong to canonical editing commands rather than feature-specific repair?",
      "action": "reopens",
      "reason": "The reported required-title heading and quote behavior and its source-matched proof stand. The implementation's canApplyBlockChanges copy is a second admission path that can drift from actual commands and requires whole-document validation on matching input."
    },
    {
      "record": "2026-09-21-editing-command-final-pass",
      "question": "Which insertion, deletion, normalization and escape rules belong to canonical editing commands rather than feature-specific repair?",
      "action": "retains",
      "reason": "Its explicit direction to keep declarative rules while making every attempt atomic and aligning decline with command outcomes directly governs this narrower follow-up."
    }
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    "Keep canApplyBlockChanges: preserves the two proven prefix fallbacks without a new public API, but duplicates the transformation, copies ancestor arrays, validates the whole document and leaves other marker-first rules exposed to silent command decline.",
    "Replace the helper with schema.canContainAt: smaller and local, but that query checks slot admissibility, not the complete candidate or the actual command result; configured wrapper properties and child grammar can still disagree.",
    "Move only the helper into Plite: rejects the Plate-owned replacement callback but preserves a shadow-document preflight and two sources of truth.",
    "Delete input rules or the canonical prefix schema: rejects current priority/composition and shared raw-editor structure jobs without solving atomic command admission.",
    "Let the actual structural operation produce an applied/declined result inside an atomic transaction attempt, and compose marker deletion only when admitted: pursue as the design direction. The detailed API and cost remain open."
  ],
  "proofLimits": "Source inspection establishes two helper callers and other marker-first structural rules, but it does not prove that the other rules currently fail on a live schema, quantify whole-document validation cost, or validate a replacement transaction primitive. The September 22 tests/browser receipt prove only the repaired required-title cases. A design must test exact commands, nested/named roots, fallback marker preservation, undo/replay and any new repeated runtime cost before adoption.",
